and the base absolute (batched ns/op), which a ratio is formed against:
  worst relative move: 7.5 % (P2.2)

## What the bar means, and what it does not

**0.078 is the bar for the DELTA instrument, not for the main bench.** `gen/drift.sh`
perturbs `ak.RunDelta`, which has no incumbent and nothing allocating between rounds, on
purpose: the main bench rebuilds a pool of protobuf messages between rounds so its baseline
is honest, and the collector that follows is already wider than most of what is being
looked for. Drifting the main bench would have measured that twice. Its ranges are wider
than this bar by construction and it prints them.

For comparison the cpp slice's equivalent is **0.240**, and it is measured over a whole
harness including its incumbent.

## Which conclusions clear it

| conclusion | effect | clears 0.078 |
|---|---|---|
| decision 9's fill on P1.3 (the absent path) | 0.68 | yes, by an order of magnitude |
| batching on P2.4 | 0.33 | yes |
| batching on P2.3 | 0.26 | yes |
| batching on P2.5 | 0.16 | yes |
| batching on P1.3 | 0.15 | yes |
| batching on P3.1 | 0.13 | yes |
| batching on P2.2 | 0.10 | yes, narrowly |
| batching on P1.2 | 0.06 | **no** |
| batching on P4.1 | 0.05 | **no** |
| batching on P1.1 | 0.02 | **no** |
| batching on P6.1 | 0.01 | **no** |
| decision 9's fill anywhere but P1.3 | 0.00 to 0.04 | **no** |

The rows that do not clear it are not thereby zero: the median is positive in all three
builds on every payload that has a repeated field, and on P1.2 the measured delta per
element is 5.9 ns against an arithmetic prediction of 10.9 (one extra forward crossing at
this machine's 11 ns). They are quoted as a sign and an arithmetic agreement, never as a
magnitude.

Decision 9 is the sharper case: it clears the bar on the absent path and nowhere else,
which is exactly the shape the decision asked about -- "wins on the absent path, costs
less than 5.4 ns per element elsewhere".
